A||||19||horseman||Rear control arm bushing replacement?||||5||
Z||000000||horseman||12-03-2000||11:18 PM||||Hey all,<BR>From what I have read, replacing the rear control arm bushings are the single most noticeable improvement that you can do for handling. Several techs reccomend poly and Herb Adams likes the metal bushings(racing only). I would like to try the poly's but, the Haynes manual says that you have to replace the whole control arm. Not just the bushing. What gives? Has anyone done this on their car? What steps are neccessary? <BR>Thanks in advance,<BR>Charlie||||reg||5
Z||000001||RNGCRSH||12-03-2000||11:23 PM||||To put in poly bushings you remove the rubber bushing from from its outter sleve by burning it out then you grease and press the polly into the original outer sleeve. you dont remove the outer bushing sleeve from the arm. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||12-03-2000||11:37 PM||||Herb Adams was talking about Cradle bushings not control arm bushings. As for control arm bushings, Just burn out or freeze out with LN2 the old bushings, Grease the polys and install.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||12-03-2000||11:42 PM||||unless you own the 88, the replacement of the cradle bushings is the single best improvment you can make. I would do that Before trying any poly anywhere else. You'll be relearning how to drive the car again after this one. It took me a week to get used to mine. the OE cradle rubber, even new, allows way to much movement. Any movment of the cradle realigns the entire rear of the car. It can change thrust angle toe and caster dynamicly. Not good things to have change in a turn.<P>Consider poly control arms very carefully. They will require maintenance or they will squeak. It's only a matter of when that will start not if. Fail to install them propperly and they will do it even sooner.||||reg||1
Z||000004||horseman||12-04-2000||12:03 AM||||Did you guys read the same Herb Adams article that I did? LOOK again theogre, as I am sure that Herb said the rear control arms and not the cradle bushings. He suggested steel bushings and welding of parts. I don't need that aggressive of an approach since my fiero will only see street driving.<BR>And please explain the procedures for burning/freezing the old bushings.<BR>Charlie||||reg||4
Z||000005||horseman||12-04-2000||12:06 AM||||Here's the address for one of the Adam's articles that I was referencing: <A HREF="http://www.homestead.com/Fiero2m4Adams.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.homestead.com/Fiero2m4Adams.html</A> <BR>This site also has other great tips on handling and fiero performance.<P>Charlie||||reg||14
Z||000006||terryk||12-04-2000||12:55 AM||||I have the Herb Adams sphericals in the rear control arms on my 86 supercharged Fiero. Nice. Very nice. I wouldn't ever pay that price for them and they take some milling/welding to install.<P>Use poly if you must but expect them to squeek. New rubber bushings will last 5 years. I recommend rubber. I fabricated the special tool for installing them. Threaded rod, large washers, two iron pipe fittings. Let me knowif you need the details.||||reg||1
Z||000007||horseman||12-04-2000||10:09 AM||||terryk,<BR>please post the details regarding your install. tool. Or email me. Thanks for the tips.<BR>Charlie||||reg||1
Z||000008||bHooper||12-04-2000||11:04 AM||||i cant seem to get that web site to work, does anyone else have a good link to info on/by Herb Adams?<P>thanks!<P><BR>also, does anyone know the diameter of the sway bars avaiable at jc whitney?<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/alien.gif">   <BR>We are not alone!||||reg||1
Z||000009||Will||12-04-2000||12:45 PM||||Has anyone tried a holesaw to remove the OEM rubber? It would have to be big enough to fit over the inner sleeve, but go inside the outer sleeve. I haven't tried it yet, but it might work better than burning them out; that gets the steel sleeves mighty hot.||||reg||1
Z||000010||N_Man||12-04-2000||12:53 PM||||Hey terry<BR>please email me details on your tool.  These control arms are fairly easy to bend if you do it wrong, yep i bent one of my fronts pressing in a bushing.  So if you could send me the details, thanks||||reg||1
Z||000011||theogre||12-04-2000||07:31 PM||||The correct link is <A HREF="http://www.homestead.com/Fiero2m4/Adams.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.homestead.com/Fiero2m4/Adams.html</A> <P>I looked at the article in question. It's been awhile since I read it. I'll look at the other articles more latter. If you read the article it's actually kind of backwards.<P>Elimination of the rubber cradle mounts is one of the big reasons 88's have better handling. Rubber cradle bushings can and will allow very large alignment changes. This isn't anything radical. It's basic common sense. If the thing the control arm connects to is moving around so is the control arm. Add in sloppy control arm bushings and the ball joints are moving every which way. Working the control arm without adressing the thing they are mounted to only fixes a portion of the problem.<P>I wasn't joking above. replace the cradle bushings and it will behave like a whole diferant car. My OE rubber was in decent shape compared to some I've seen but it was still all over the place. With poly in the cradle the rear is so much more stable that I'll be putting in new OE rubber when the time comes for that. Maybe if I had one of the big V6 motors or a V8 I'd do polly. I'd never do that steel on steel thing to a street car.<P><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 12-04-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||batboy||12-04-2000||09:20 PM||||It's been a long time since I've read Herb's article, but like Ogre, I'm thinking he's talking about the cradle bushings. Don't put steel bushing in the control arms if this is a street driven car...jeez. I have poly bushings in front and rear control arms and on the sway bar. If you do it right, it won't squeak. If it does, turn up the stereo. Mine don't make noise and I love the handling. I used a hacksaw blade to cut one shallow groove into the inside bolt hole. This groove traps some of the special grease that you're supposed to use when you install them instead of letting it all squeeze out when you install them. Keeps them lubed for a long time. Still, I wish someone would make graphite impregnated poly bushings for the Fiero. Those would never squeak.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 12-05-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||theogre||12-04-2000||11:33 PM||||I read it just before I posted earlier. I don't know if it's the way its written or if he actually took the aproach as written. It comes off backwards, like they are recomending doing the arms and then maybe the cradle.<P>To put it this way...... would you build a house on a rubber foundation? That's what a stock 84-87 Fiero cradle is. A rubber foundation. Everything attached to it moves with it and may add more movement to that. ||||reg||1
Z||000014||the need for speed||12-05-2000||12:06 AM||||how do you do the maintenance on the poly?if you dont like them can they be removed with out damage,like the rubber one's were you have to burn them out?||||reg||1
Z||000015||msweldon||12-05-2000||09:06 AM||||I have the Herb Adams needle bearings in my rear A-arms on my 87gt, and like terryk said, They are very nice, pricey but nice.  From what I've researched both the rear a-arm bushings and cradle bushings allow too much movement.  The a-arm bushings can allow as much as 1 inch of deflection between the rear wheels in hard acceleration and turning.  After helping my local speed shop replace the bushings, they were a pain to install too, the car totally changed its handling characteristics.  And I too had to re-learn how to drive the car, it simply goes where you point it, too well at times.  Yes, I still have cradle movement which does affect cornering stability but the threshold upon which I reach this instability is much higher.<P>As for ride, I replaced the rubber up front with poly sway bar mounts and links front and rear with the needle bushings in the rear lower a-arms.  Yes, the ride was more harsh, but no more so than a rear poly'd fiero I had driven before.<P>I do plan on installing solid cradle mounts after for my 3.4 dohc install I'm currently getting into, but we shall see if the ride becomes too harsh at that point.<P>thanks,<BR>marc||||reg||1
Z||000016||bHooper||12-05-2000||09:47 AM||||After reading his article, I interpreted it as to read that he suggested using steal bushings on the A arms.<P>He refers to the cradle as the "subframe" and  says that you can use steal or aluminum bushings for this, but "The handling improvements gained by this modification are less than when the control arm rubber is removed"<P>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000017||horseman||12-06-2000||01:24 AM||||Hey Hoop,<BR>Thanks for confirming that. I thought maybe I had misinterpreted Mr.Adams after reading all of the response posts.<BR>I received email from another fanatic who autocrosses his car;he says the handling was dramatically improved with steel bushings in the arms and ride quality was still good.<BR>Charlie||||reg||14
Z||000018||batboy||12-06-2000||06:51 AM||||Again, as you noted Horsey, Herb says this is racing stuff. The guy who likes them is doing SCCA racing. You said that your car is street driven only. You need a little bit a give and take for a daily driver. Rubber is too sloppy for good solid performance driving. Poly is an excellent compromise.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/batkid3.gif">   <B>BATBOY</B><BR>1985 SE auto with 350 Chevy V8 (Batmobile),<BR>suspension mods, lowered, GA brakes, etc...||||reg||1
Z||000019||terryk||12-06-2000||09:59 AM||||I'll get the parts out and measure them. <P>The main point is that you need to put tension on the bushings, then dent the metal sleeve until it pops out. Then you need a split sleeve to put between the loops to keep them from compressing when you install the bushing. Otherwise, the bushing may bind. <P>I use a bit of vaseline and they slide right in and then seat. I then clean that off with brake cleaner. I've never had one come out.<P>Terry||||reg||1
